.th LP IV 5/27/74
.sh NAME
lp \*- line printer
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Lp
provides the interface to any of the standard
DEC line printers.
When it is opened or closed, a suitable number
of page ejects is generated.
Bytes written are printed.
.s3
An internal parameter within the driver determines
whether or not the device is treated as having
a 96- or 64-character set.
In half-ASCII mode, 
certain characters are escaped according to
the following table:
.s3
.if t .ig
.lp +10 5
{	(-
.lp +10 5
}	)-
.lp +10 5
\*g	\*a-
.lp +10 5
|	!-
.lp +10 5
~	^-
..
.if n .ig
.lp +10 5
{	\o"(\*-"
.lp +10 5
}	\o")\*-"
.lp +10 5
\*g	\o"\*a\*-"
.lp +10 5
|	\o"!\*-"
.lp +10 5
~	\o"^\*-"
..
.i0
.s3
No case conversion of alphabetics is performed.
.s3
Writing a 377 byte or a 376 byte will cause the driver to generate
a series of blanks or zeroes respectively, according to the following
byte count.
Writing a 375 byte will cause the page length to be set to 32767
(i.e. infinity).
.s3
KRONOS
.it eor
(end of record - ctl/r) and
.it eof
(end of file - ctl/f) characters cause a form feed.
.s3
The driver correctly interprets
carriage returns, backspaces, tabs, and form feeds.
A series of newlines or formfeeds on the first line of a page
is ignored.
The driver will cause a skip to the page boundary on receipt of a
"vertical tab" character (013).
Lines longer than 136 characters are truncated.
.s3
These parameters (some of many) may be set using
.it slp
(I).
.sh FILES
/dev/lp
.sh "SEE ALSO"
lpr (I), slp (I)
.sh BUGS
Not very bright about an escape character being the last byte
transmitted.
Does get itself into a funny state sometimes.
